#. Tag: title
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:2
#, no-c-format
msgid "Colorspaces"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:4
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This chapter gives information on what colorspaces are, which colorspaces "
"&krita; offers, and what you should keep in mind when using them."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:10
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction to colorspaces"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:13
#, no-c-format
msgid "What is a colorspace?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:15
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In short, a colorspace is a way to represent colors by specifying a number "
"of parameters. As parameters, one can choose for example the amounts of red, "
"green and blue light needed for the color. This results in the commonly "
"known RGB colorspace. One can visualize this as a three-dimensional space, "
"with each of the red, green, and blue light components being an axis in the "
"colorspace. A color then corresponds to a certain point in this colorspace, "
"defined by its coordinates on the three axes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:24
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To be more precise, a colorspace is a combination of a color model "
"(indicating which axes are present) and a mapping function (indicating which "
"values correspond to which colors)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:29
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Not every color can be represented in every colorspace. Some colorspaces "
"define more, or different, colors than others. The set of colors that can be "
"represented in a certain colorspace is called its gamut. Because gamuts can "
"differ widely, it is not guaranteed that images in a certain colorspace can "
"be converted to another colorspace without having to substitute certain "
"colors for others, even if they are based on the same color model."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:43
#, no-c-format
msgid "Available colorspaces"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:45
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&krita; offers colorspaces based on RGB, CMYK, Lab, LMS, YCbCr, and Gray "
"color models. These are shortly discussed in this section."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:51
#, no-c-format
msgid "The RGB color models"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:53
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The abbreviation RGB stands for Red, Green, Blue, and the color model with "
"this name refers to the three light components that are emitted in displays "
"(televisions, computer monitors, etcetera) to create a certain color. This "
"color model is used by default in virtually any standard painting "
"application."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:58
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When defining a color in the RGB model, its red, green and blue components "
"are specified. If all components are absent (each component is emitted at 0 "
"percent intensity, so no light at all), the color is pure black. If all "
"components are fully present (100 percent intensity), the color is pure "
"white. If one component is present at full intensity and the other two are "
"absent, the pure respective color is obtained."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:65
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Two more examples: if both red and green are emitted at 100 percent and blue "
"is not emitted, pure yellow is obtained. A color with all three components "
"at the same intensity is a shade of gray."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There are various colorspaces that implement the RGB model. For example, the "
"so-called RGB8 colorspace represents each color with 8 bits per component. "
"Since 8 bits allow for 256 distinct values, the total number of different "
"colors that can be specified in this colorspace is 256 (red) * 256 (green) * "
"256 (blue), or about 16.7 million colors. In &krita;, a couple of RGB "
"colorspaces are available, for example RGB32, which is able to distinguish "
"between 4.2 billion values per component."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:82
#, no-c-format
msgid "The CMYK color model"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:84
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"CMYK is the abbreviation for Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, blacK (although "
"officially the K stands for Key, black is much more commonly used). This "
"color model is based on ink: a color is specified by the amount of ink "
"needed for a point to be perceived as having that color."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:89
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Since CMYK colors are used by printers while RGB colors are used on-screen, "
"one often wants to convert RGB colors to CMYK colors. As this cannot always "
"be done correctly, printed images may turn out to look quite different than "
"what is perceived on-screen."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:99
#, no-c-format
msgid "The L*a*b* color model"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:101
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This color model uses three parameters for a color: its luminance or "
"lightness (L*, which lies between 0 for black and 100 for white), its "
"position between absolute red and absolute green (a*, which is negative for "
"colors closer to green and positive for colors closer to red), and its "
"position between yellow and blue (b*, which is negative for colors closer to "
"blue and positive for colors closer to yellow)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:113
#, no-c-format
msgid "The LMS color model"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:115
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This model is based on the contribution of actual light wave lengths to the "
"color. The human eye is sensitive to three types of light waves, "
"distinguished by their wave lengths: long (L), middle (M) and short (S) "
"waves. The eye's sensitivity for a certain color on these three wavelengths "
"can be expressed in L, M and S coordinates."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:126
#, no-c-format
msgid "The YCbCr color model"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:128
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The YCbCr model is often used for video systems. The Y parameter indicates "
"the luminance or lightness of the color (which can be seen as a gray-tone), "
"the Cb and Cr parameters indicate the chrominance (color tone): Cb places "
"the color on a scale between blue and yellow, Cr indicates the place of the "
"color between red and green."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:138
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Gray color model"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-colorspaces.docbook:140
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Gray color model simply represents colors as shades of gray (with black "
"and white being the extremes)."
msgstr ""

